                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Forager: Adorable at First, Exhausting by the Endgame[/h1]\n\nForager is such an incredibly cute and enjoyable game when you first start playing. Exploring, gathering resources, unlocking new areas, and watching your little world grow is genuinely satisfying. It's the kind of game that makes you want to keep playing just a little longer.\n\nBut things start to feel different when you reach the endgame.\n\n[h1]Cute, Cozy, and Addictive[/h1]\n\nThe early game is where Forager really shines for me. There's always something new to unlock, something to collect, or another upgrade to work towards. It's a simple concept, but it works surprisingly well.\n\n[h1]The Endgame Grind[/h1]\n\nThe biggest problem comes when you're trying to farm the last few achievements. At that point, the experience becomes much less enjoyable, especially when everything grows back so quickly and gets in the way of what you're trying to do.\n\nThe Lightning Rod also makes farming almost too easy, which takes away some of the satisfaction of working towards those final achievements.\n\nAnd then there's fishing...\n\n[h1]Fishing and Me Don't Get Along[/h1]\n\nLet's just say that fishing and I aren't exactly friends. Maybe I'm just terrible at it, but getting through that part of the game was not something I particularly enjoyed.\n\n[h1]Conclusion[/h1]\n\nForager is a genuinely charming little game, and I had a great time with it, especially in the beginning. However, the endgame grind can become frustrating when you're chasing those last few achievements.\n\nI'd still recommend it if you enjoy relaxing resource-gathering games with plenty of progression. Just be prepared for the endgame to test your patience a little more than you'd expect.\n\n[h1]Rating: 8/10[/h1]",
